// Requests the WAF BLOCKED on an authentication path, with the time, the client, the host and
// the rule that did it. The query that answers "are we blocking real customers?".
//
// Loaded by Search-MsecLogAnalytics -Subject Waf -Name BlockedAuth -WorkspaceName <ws>.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. A blocked login is the most expensive WAF false positive there is: the
// customer cannot get in, they do not report it as a security problem, and it is invisible in
// any view that groups by rule or by volume - six blocked logins hide inside a day of ordinary
// scanner noise. Grouping by PATH instead surfaces them immediately.
//
// EXPECT THE BLOCKING RULE TO BE 949110, NOT THE RULE THAT CAUSED IT. CRS 3.x is anomaly
// scoring: 949110 "Inbound Anomaly Score Exceeded" is what logs Action="Blocked", while the
// rules that drove the score logged only Action="Matched". So this tells you THAT a login was
// blocked; to find out WHY, join on TransactionId and read every Matched row for it. The
// contributing rules are often not the one anybody excluded.
//
// A BLOCK HERE AFTER AN EXCLUSION WAS DEPLOYED MEANS THE EXCLUSION DID NOT WORK, or did not
// cover everything feeding the score. Compare the timestamp against the release time rather
// than against the date - a same-day block before the deployment proves nothing either way.
// No time filter - the window is -Days / -Timespan, applied server-side. See ExclusionCheck.
AGWFirewallLogs
| where Action == "Blocked"
| where RequestUri has_any ("login", "signin", "sign-in", "authorize", "OnSuccessLogin",
                            "account", "token", "oauth", "saml")
| project TimeGenerated, Hostname, ClientIp, RuleId, RequestUri, TransactionId, Message
| order by TimeGenerated desc
| take 200
